= Geometric group theory
{wiki}

Geometric group theory studies finitely generated groups through their actions on metric spaces and through the large-scale geometry of spaces such as their Cayley graphs.

= Combing of a group
{parent=Geometric group theory}

= Combing
{synonym}

Choose a finite symmetric generating alphabet $\Sigma$ and one word $\sigma(g)$ representing each group element, with $\sigma(1)$ the empty word. The associated <paths> in the <Cayley graph> start at the identity and traverse one edge per unit time, remaining at their endpoint after completion. A synchronous <combing> has a constant $K$ such that <paths> to adjacent endpoints stay within $K$ at equal times. An asynchronous version permits increasing reparametrizations. The <formal language> need not be a <regular language>; regular synchronous representative languages define <automatic groups>.

= Combable group
{parent=Combing of a group}

A <finitely generated group> is synchronously combable if it admits a <combing of a group> with the <synchronous fellow-traveller property>. Without a regular-language assumption, this is more general than an <automatic group>. A bounded-rung strip argument gives a <finite group presentation>, and finite-state row shortening gives a recursive exponential filling bound and a soluble <word problem for a group>.

= Finite presentation from a synchronous combing
{parent=Combable group}

Attach <relators> for every identity word of length at most $2K+2$, where $K$ is an integer fellow-traveller constant. For any null loop, connect its consecutive <combing> <paths> at integer heights by rungs of length at most $K$. Each resulting quadrilateral has perimeter at most $2K+2$ and thus is a <relator>. The bottom row is collapsed at the identity and the top row is the original loop, so these finitely many <relators> fill every null word and present the group.

= Finite-state row shortening of a combing diagram
{parent=Finite presentation from a synchronous combing}

For a null loop of length $n$, a horizontal row of its <combing> diagram is encoded by an ordered $n$-tuple of rung words of length at most $K$. If there are $D$ such words, there are at most $D^n$ row types. Between two identical labelled rows one can delete the entire intervening annular band and glue the equal boundary <paths>. Repeating this leaves at most $D^n$ layers of $n$ bounded-perimeter cells. Thus a synchronous <combable group> has a recursive exponential isoperimetric bound. This does not require the <combing> language to be regular or computable.

= Synchronous fellow-traveller property
{parent=Combing of a group}

Two <paths> satisfy the synchronous fellow-traveller property with constant $K$ if $d(p(t),q(t))\le K$ for all equal times, with completed <paths> held at their endpoints. Checking integer times suffices up to increasing $K$ by at most two for unit-speed edge <paths>. A synchronous <combing of a group> requires a uniform constant for all adjacent endpoints.

= Automatic group
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

An <automatic group> admits an <automatic structure for a group>: a <regular language> of representatives and synchronous <group multiplier automata> recognizing right multiplication by each generator and equality of representatives. Representatives need not be unique. This finite-state description yields effective algorithms for the <word problem for a group>.

= Automatic structure for a group
{parent=Automatic group}
{title2=$(A,L)$}

An <automatic structure for a group> consists of a finite generating <alphabet> $A$, a surjective representative <regular language> $L\subseteq A^*$, and <finite-state automata> accepting the <padded convolutions of words> $(u,v)$ in $L$ satisfying $\overline u x=\overline v$ for each $x\in A\cup\{\varepsilon\}$. Symmetry of $A$ and uniqueness of representatives are additional conditions, not part of this definition.

= Group multiplier automaton
{parent=Automatic structure for a group}
{title2=$M_x$}

A <group multiplier automaton> accepts exactly the <padded convolutions of words> $u\otimes v$ with $u,v\in L$ and $\overline u x=\overline v$. The case $x=\varepsilon$ recognizes equality in the <group>, rather than equality of literal <words over an alphabet>.

= Bounded-difference automaton
{parent=Group multiplier automaton}
{title2=$d\mapsto u^{-1}dv$}

For a fixed finite generating alphabet and integer $K$, use the elements of the word-metric ball $B_K(1)$ as states, together with a reject state and padding-status flags. Starting at the identity, a padded letter pair $(u,v)$ changes the state to $u^{-1}dv$ if it stays in the ball. A padding letter acts as the identity. Final state $a$ recognizes endpoint difference $a$ among pairs whose prefixes remain within $K$. On a regular synchronous <combing>, intersecting with the two word acceptors gives the full <group multiplier automaton>. It does not recognize unrestricted endpoint relations for all input words.

= Linear-time multiplication in an automatic structure
{parent=Group multiplier automaton}
{title2=$|v|\leq|u|+N$}

Fix the first tape of a <group multiplier automaton> and use <dynamic programming> over automaton states and output-padding status, retaining predecessor pointers. The <pumping of a group multiplier automaton> bounds the search by $|u|+N$ layers, each of fixed size. This constructs a representative of $\overline u x$ in time $O(|u|+1)$ without assuming uniqueness of representatives.

= Pumping of a group multiplier automaton
{parent=Group multiplier automaton}

In an accepted <padded convolution of words>, a loop after one tape ends can be deleted or repeated while keeping that entire tape fixed. The altered other tape still represents the same required neighbouring <group> element. A shortest representative has a suffix no longer than the fixed number of automaton states; an accepted longer suffix gives infinitely many representatives of the same element.

= Small cancellation theory
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

Small cancellation theory controls overlap between <relators> or attaching paths of polygonal two-cells. Requiring overlaps to be small compared with cell perimeters gives strong <disc diagram> structure, shortening algorithms and geometric restrictions on subgroups. The <metric small cancellation condition> $C'(1/6)$ is a central example.

= Subcomplex with no missing shells
{parent=Small cancellation theory}

A <CW subcomplex> has no missing shells if a cell with a shell-length boundary arc contained in the <CW subcomplex> is itself contained in the <CW subcomplex>. For $C'(1/6)$ it suffices that every excluded cell have a complementary boundary arc of length at least half its perimeter with no edge in the <CW subcomplex>: an arc lying in the <CW subcomplex> then has length at most half and cannot be a shell's exterior arc.

= Greendlinger lemma
{parent=Small cancellation theory}
{c}

= Greendlinger theorem
{c}
{synonym}

= Greendlinger's lemma
{c}
{synonym}

For the <metric small cancellation condition> $C'(1/6)$, every nonempty freely reduced <null-homotopic> combinatorial word contains a segment of a cell boundary longer than half its perimeter. Such a segment can be replaced by the shorter complementary segment. The diagram explanation uses shells with at most three internal pieces: their internal length is less than half the perimeter. Degenerate diagrams require the <disc diagram spur> alternative.

= Greendlinger ladder theorem
{parent=Greendlinger lemma}
{c}

A reduced $C'(1/6)$ <disc diagram> is a single vertex, a single closed cell, a <disc diagram ladder>, or contains at least three exposed <disc diagram spurs> and/or <disc diagram shells> with at most three interior pieces. This is the strong diagram form of the <Greendlinger lemma>. It follows from the combinatorial curvature inequality after suppressing valence-two vertices: if there are not three positively curved exposed features, all remaining blocks must occur in a chain. In a nontrivial multi-block ladder its two ends are exposed features. Marking two boundary corners and excluding shells/spurs in the side interiors therefore forces the ladder alternative. This is the width-one case of \a[https://web.math.ucsb.edu/~jon.mccammond/papers/fanladder.pdf][McCammond and Wise, Theorem 9.4]; reduced $C'(1/6)$ diagrams satisfy its arc-reduced $C(6)$–$T(3)$ hypotheses.

= Metric small cancellation condition
{parent=Small cancellation theory}
{title2=$C'(\lambda)$}

The condition $C'(\lambda)$ requires that every <piece in small cancellation theory> occurring in a cell boundary have length strictly less than $\lambda$ times that cell's perimeter. Length counts edges. For $\lambda=1/6$, an inner path made of at most three pieces has length strictly less than half the perimeter, so the complementary outer path is strictly longer. The attaching paths are combinatorial immersions and the piece convention is part of the chosen presentation or polygonal complex.

= Piece in small cancellation theory
{parent=Small cancellation theory}

= Small cancellation piece
{synonym}

A piece is a nontrivial combinatorial path that occurs in two essentially distinct positions on oriented cell attaching paths. These may be different cells or distinct admissible occurrences on one attaching path; occurrences identified by the defining attaching-path symmetry are not counted twice. In a symmetrized <group presentation>, it is a common initial segment of distinct <relator> words. Internal arcs of a <reduced disc diagram> are pieces, because otherwise the neighboring cells form a cancellable pair.

= Cobounded group action
{parent=Geometric group theory}

An isometric <group action> on a <metric space> is cobounded if some orbit is within a fixed distance $R$ of every point. Equivalently, translates of one bounded ball cover the space. Coboundedness does not require finite stabilizers, a proper space, or a finitely generated group. Every action on a point is cobounded.

= CAT(0) space
{c}
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

A CAT(0) space is a geodesic metric space whose geodesic triangles are no thicker than their Euclidean comparison triangles.

= Hadamard space
{c}
{parent=CAT(0) space}

A <Hadamard space> is a complete <CAT(0) space>. Equivalently, every pair has a midpoint $m$ satisfying the midpoint inequality $d(z,m)^2\leq\tfrac12d(z,x)^2+\tfrac12d(z,y)^2-\tfrac14d(x,y)^2$ for every $z$. This inequality makes the midpoint unique. Iterating midpoints and using completeness constructs the unique <geodesic> joining the pair. The squared-distance inequality extends to every point of that <geodesic> by dyadic approximation and implies Euclidean triangle comparison and <geodesic convexity> of closed balls.

= Hadamard squared-distance inequality
{c}
{parent=Hadamard space}
{title2=$d(z,\gamma(t))^2\leq(1-t)d(z,x)^2+td(z,y)^2-t(1-t)d(x,y)^2$}

For the constant-speed <geodesic> $\gamma$ from $x$ to $y$ in a <Hadamard space>, the displayed inequality follows by repeatedly applying the midpoint inequality and passing from dyadic parameters to all $t\in[0,1]$. Applying it on two sides of a triangle gives $d(x_s,y_t)^2\leq s^2A^2+t^2B^2-st(A^2+B^2-C^2)$, where $A=d(a,x)$, $B=d(a,y)$ and $C=d(x,y)$. The right side is the squared comparison distance by the Euclidean cosine rule.

= Cubical complex
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

= Cube complex
{synonym}

A cube complex is formed by gluing Euclidean cubes along isometric faces so that intersecting cubes meet along common faces.

= Link of a vertex in a cube complex
{parent=Cubical complex}

The link of a vertex $v$ records the local directions from $v$: its vertices are incident edge germs, and a collection spans a simplex exactly when the corresponding edge germs lie in a common cube.

= Nonpositively curved cube complex
{parent=Cubical complex}

A cube complex is nonpositively curved when every vertex link is a <flag simplicial complex>. Equivalently, its universal cover with the piecewise Euclidean metric is a <CAT(0) space>.

= CAT(0) cube complex
{c}
{parent=Nonpositively curved cube complex}

A CAT(0) cube complex is a simply connected <nonpositively curved cube complex>.

= Hyperplane of a cube complex
{parent=Cubical complex}

Two edges of a cube complex are parallel when they are opposite sides of a square, with transitive closure understood. A hyperplane is assembled from the midcubes dual to one parallelism class of edges.

= Carrier of a hyperplane
{parent=Hyperplane of a cube complex}

The carrier $N(H)$ of a hyperplane $H$ is the union of the closed cubes that meet $H$.

= Special cube complex
{parent=Cubical complex}
{wiki}

A nonpositively curved cube complex is special when every hyperplane is embedded and two-sided, no hyperplane self-osculates, and no two hyperplanes interosculate. Equivalently, it admits a cubical local isometry to a <Salvetti complex>.

= Self-intersecting hyperplane
{parent=Special cube complex}

A hyperplane self-intersects when two adjacent edges of one square are dual to that same hyperplane.

= One-sided hyperplane
{parent=Special cube complex}

A hyperplane is one-sided when its carrier is a twisted interval bundle rather than a product with an interval.

= Self-osculating hyperplane
{parent=Special cube complex}

A two-sided hyperplane self-osculates when two distinct, consistently oriented edges dual to it have the same initial vertex but do not form the corner of a square.

= Interosculating hyperplanes
{parent=Special cube complex}

Two hyperplanes interosculate when they cross in one square and also have dual edges meeting at a vertex without spanning a square elsewhere.

= Fundamental group of a special cube complex
{parent=Special cube complex}

The local isometry from a connected special cube complex to a <Salvetti complex> induces an injection of fundamental groups. Hence its fundamental group is a subgroup of a <right-angled Artin group>.

= Wallspace
{parent=Cubical complex}

A wallspace $(S,\mathcal W)$ is a set $S$ with walls $W=\{W^+,W^-\}$, each a partition into two halfspaces, such that only finitely many walls separate any two points of $S$.

Such wall partitions encode separation data used to construct a dual <cube complex>.

= Wall
{parent=Wallspace}

A wall is an unordered pair of complementary <halfspace>[halfspaces] $\{W^+,W^-\}$.

= Halfspace
{parent=Wall}
{wiki}

A halfspace is one of the two complementary parts determined by a wall.

= Crossing walls
{parent=Wall}

Two walls cross when all four intersections of one halfspace from each wall are nonempty. A collection of pairwise crossing walls is also called transverse.

= Transverse wall collection
{parent=Crossing walls}

= Transverse collection of walls
{synonym}

A transverse collection of walls is a collection in which every two distinct walls cross.

= Wall metric
{title2=$d_{\mathcal W}$}
{parent=Wallspace}

The wall metric is
$$
d_{\mathcal W}(x,y)=\#\{W\in\mathcal W:W\text{ separates }x\text{ and }y\}.
$$

= Dual cube complex of a wallspace
{title2=$C(S,\mathcal W)$}
{parent=Wallspace}

The vertices of the dual cube complex are consistent choices of one halfspace of every wall that differ from a principal choice on only finitely many walls. Two vertices are joined when their choices differ on one wall, and higher cubes fill the resulting hypercubes. The result is a <CAT(0) cube complex>.

= Principal vertex of a dual cube complex
{parent=Dual cube complex of a wallspace}

A point $x\in S$ determines a principal vertex by choosing, for every wall, the halfspace containing $x$. The combinatorial distance between the principal vertices of $x$ and $y$ is $d_{\mathcal W}(x,y)$.

= Cubulation of a group
{parent=Dual cube complex of a wallspace}

A cubulation of a group is a metrically proper action by cubical automorphisms on a <CAT(0) cube complex>. It is cocompact when the action is also a <cocompact group action>.

= Metrically proper group action
{parent=Cubulation of a group}

An isometric group action on a metric space is metrically proper when, for some and hence every point $x$, the set $\{g:d(x,gx)\le R\}$ is finite for every finite $R$.

= Wall-metric properness criterion
{parent=Cubulation of a group}

If $G$ acts on $(S,\mathcal W)$ and $x\in S$, then its action on the dual cube complex is metrically proper whenever
$$
d_{\mathcal W}(x,gx)\longrightarrow\infty
$$
as $g$ leaves every finite subset of $G$.

= Transverse-wall cocompactness criterion
{parent=Cubulation of a group}

The action of $G$ on the dual cube complex is cocompact exactly when there are finitely many $G$-orbits of finite transverse wall collections. Equivalently, there is a uniform bound on their sizes and, for every size, only finitely many orbits.

= Right-angled Artin group
{title2=$A_\Gamma$}
{c}
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

= RAAG
{c}
{synonym}

For a <graph> $\Gamma$, the right-angled Artin group is
$$
A_\Gamma=\left\langle v\in V(\Gamma)\mathrel{\middle|}[v,w]=1\text{ whenever }\{v,w\}\in E(\Gamma)\right\rangle.
$$

= Salvetti complex
{title2=$S_\Gamma$}
{c}
{parent=Right-angled Artin group}
{wiki}

The Salvetti complex $S_\Gamma$ has one vertex, one oriented loop for each vertex of $\Gamma$, and an $n$-torus cubulated by one $n$-cube for every $n$-clique of $\Gamma$. It is nonpositively curved and $\pi_1S_\Gamma\cong A_\Gamma$.

= Vertex link of a Salvetti complex
{parent=Salvetti complex}

The unique vertex of $S_\Gamma$ has two link vertices $v^+,v^-$ for each $v\in V(\Gamma)$. Signed vertices $v_1^{\epsilon_1},\ldots,v_k^{\epsilon_k}$ span a simplex exactly when the distinct underlying vertices $v_1,\ldots,v_k$ form a clique of $\Gamma$.

= Hyperplane of a Salvetti complex
{parent=Salvetti complex}

The hyperplane of $S_\Gamma$ dual to the loop labelled $v$ is naturally the Salvetti complex of the <induced subgraph> on the neighbours of $v$.

= Residual finiteness of a right-angled Artin group
{parent=Right-angled Artin group}

Every right-angled Artin group is a <residually finite group>. One route is its faithful integral linear representation, followed by reduction modulo suitable primes.

= Free group
{title2=$F_n$}
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

The free group $F(S)$ on a set $S$ consists of reduced words in letters $s^{\pm1}$, with multiplication followed by cancellation. Every map from $S$ to a group extends uniquely to a homomorphism from $F(S)$.

= Finite-index image of a free-group endomorphism is surjective
{parent=Free group}

An <endomorphism> of $F_n$ has image generated by at most $n$ elements. If this image has finite index $d$, the <Nielsen–Schreier formula> gives rank $1+d(n-1)$. Its <abelianization> requires at least that many generators. For $n\geq2$, comparison with $n$ forces $d=1$.

// Target: geometry-and-topology.bigb

= Free conjugate bases in a rank-two free group
{parent=Free group}
{title2=$b^{-i}ab^i$}

In $F(a,b)$, any set of distinct conjugates $b^{-i}ab^i$ freely generates its subgroup. Combine adjacent powers of the same conjugate in a reduced word. After expansion, distinct successive indices leave nonzero intervening powers of $b$, and the intervening nonzero powers of $a$ cannot cancel in the <free product> $\langle a\rangle*\langle b\rangle$. The same argument works with $a,b$ interchanged and for finite or countably infinite sets of indices.

= Conjugates of one free generator freely generate
{parent=Free group}

If $a,b$ freely generate, then $a^iba^{-i}$ for any distinct integer indices freely generate their subgroup. Group a reduced word in these new generators into blocks with nonzero exponents and different successive indices. Expansion produces alternating nonzero powers of $b$ and interior powers $a^{i_{j+1}-i_j}$, so it cannot freely reduce to the empty word. Consequently every finite-rank free group embeds in a rank-two free group; free rank is not monotone under subgroup inclusion.

= Freeness detected by a quotient
{parent=Free group}

Suppose a <group homomorphism> $\pi:G\to Q$ is injective on a subgroup freely generated by $x_1,\ldots,x_n$. If $y_i$ have $\pi(y_i)=\pi(x_i)$, then the $y_i$ freely generate their subgroup: a relation among them projects to a relation among the free basis images, so the corresponding abstract reduced word must be empty. In particular, this applies to $y_i=x_ig$ when the <normal closure> of $g$ intersects $\langle x_1,\ldots,x_n\rangle$ trivially.

= Free basis of a group
{parent=Free group}

= Free bases of a group
{synonym}

A subset $X$ is a free basis of a <group> $G$ when the natural map from the <free group> on $X$ to $G$ is an <isomorphism>. Equivalently, $X$ generates $G$ and no nonempty freely reduced word in $X\cup X^{-1}$ equals the identity. A generating set is not automatically a free basis.

= Freely reduced word
{parent=Free group}

A word in generators and their formal inverses is freely reduced if no consecutive letters are mutual inverses. Deleting such inverse pairs gives the unique reduced representative in a <free group>. In a general <group presentation>, <free reduction> preserves the represented group element but may not produce a shortest representative.

= Free reduction
{parent=Freely reduced word}

<Free reduction> removes adjacent inverse-letter pairs. A stack algorithm scans the letters, canceling the top letter when the next is its inverse. The resulting reduced word is unique: deleting an inverse pair before scanning does not change the final stack, so every cancellation sequence gives the same result. In any <group presentation> these deletions preserve the represented element, and in a <free group> the reduced word is the identity exactly when it is empty.

= Cyclically reduced word
{parent=Freely reduced word}

A <freely reduced word> is cyclically reduced if its first and last letters are not inverse, with the empty word also allowed. Every cyclic permutation of a nonempty <cyclically reduced word> is freely reduced, represents a conjugate, and has the same length. Positive powers are freely reduced because no cancellation occurs at copy boundaries.

= Reduced word in a free group
{parent=Free group}

= Reduced words in a free group
{synonym}

= Reduced word
{synonym}

A <reduced word> contains no adjacent generator and inverse-generator pair. Cancel such pairs to obtain the unique reduced representative in a <free group>. One proof constructs the free <group> itself from <reduced words>, with multiplication given by concatenation followed by cancellation; its action by these operations on words distinguishes distinct reduced representatives.

= Universal property of a free group
{parent=Free group}

For every function $f:S\to G$ from a set into a <group>, there is a unique <group homomorphism> $\bar f:F(S)\to G$ whose restriction to $S$ is $f$.

= Ping-pong lemma
{parent=Free group}
{wiki}

= Klein combination theorem
{c}
{synonym}

= Klein's combination theorem
{c}
{synonym}

Suppose nontrivial <groups> $G_1,G_2$ act on a <set> and there are disjoint nonempty <subsets> $X_1,X_2$ such that every nonidentity element of $G_1$ maps $X_2$ into $X_1$, while every nonidentity element of $G_2$ maps $X_1$ into $X_2$. Assume at least one factor has at least three elements. Then the subgroup generated by $G_1$ and $G_2$ is their <free product>. The cardinality hypothesis excludes the counterexample in which $G_1=G_2=C_2$ acts by swapping $X_1$ and $X_2$. Versions allowing both factors to have order two need an additional separation hypothesis.

= Residual finiteness of a free group
{parent=Free group}

Every finitely generated <free group> is a <residually finite group>. For $F_2$, the <ping-pong lemma> embeds it into $\operatorname{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$, and <reduction modulo a prime in an integral matrix group> separates each nonidentity element in a finite quotient.

= Group presentation
{title2=$\langle S\mid R\rangle$}
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

A group presentation $\langle S\mid R\rangle$ describes the quotient of the <free group> $F(S)$ by the normal closure of the relators $R$. It is finite when both $S$ and $R$ are finite.

= Recursive presentation of a group
{parent=Group presentation}

= Recursively presented group
{synonym}

A recursive presentation has an effectively enumerable alphabet of generators and a <recursively enumerable set> of relators. Usually the alphabet is finite; when it is countably infinite this is specified explicitly. Enumerating finite products of conjugates of relators and their inverses enumerates all null words. Conversely the enumerable null words can themselves serve as the relators. Recursive presentability does not imply a soluble <word problem for a group>.

= Higman's embedding theorem
{c}
{parent=Recursive presentation of a group}
{wiki}

= Higman embedding theorem
{c}
{synonym}

A <finitely generated group> embeds in a <finitely presented group> if and only if it has a <recursive presentation of a group>. The necessity follows by enumerating proofs that substituted generator words are trivial in the larger <finite group presentation>. The sufficiency is the embedding theorem; it also applies to effectively countably generated recursive presentations after an effective two-generator embedding. This is the theorem of https://doi.org/10.1098/rspa.1961.0132[Subgroups of finitely presented groups].

= p-deficiency
{parent=Group presentation}
{title2=$\operatorname{def}_p\langle X\mid R\rangle$}

For a <group presentation> $\mathcal P=\langle X\mid R\rangle$ with finitely many generators and a <prime number> $p$, define
$$
\nu_p(r)=\max\{k\geq0:r=w^{p^k}\text{ in }F(X)\},\qquad\operatorname{def}_p(\mathcal P)=|X|-\sum_{r\in R}p^{-\nu_p(r)}.
$$
This uses the unshifted convention. Identity relators have zero weight, and a divergent sum gives value $-\infty$. Some literature subtracts one instead; inequalities must be shifted accordingly. Taking roots in the <free group> matters: roots appearing only after passing to the quotient do not change the relator weight. The <p-rank of a group> bounds this quantity above, and the <index-p rewriting bound for p-deficiency> gives the infinitude criterion <p-deficiency at least one implies infinitude>.

= Torsion group construction by p-power relators
{parent=p-deficiency}

Enumerate all nonidentity words $w_i$ in the rank-two <free group> and impose relations $w_i^{p^{i+2}}=1$. The resulting two-generated <group> has
$$
\operatorname{def}_p\geq2-\sum_{i\geq1}p^{-(i+2)}=2-\frac1{p^2(p-1)}>1.
$$
It is infinite by <p-deficiency at least one implies infinitude>. Each element has order a power of $p$, so it is a <torsion group>. The infinitely many relators are essential to this particular construction; finite generation does not imply a <finite group presentation>.

= p-deficiency at least one implies infinitude
{parent=p-deficiency}

If a <group presentation> has unshifted <p-deficiency> at least one, the <p-rank of a group> is at least one and gives a normal subgroup of index $p$. The <index-p rewriting bound for p-deficiency> gives this subgroup another presentation with p-deficiency at least one. Iterating produces subgroups of index $p^j$ for every $j$, so the group is infinite. This criterion applies even with infinitely many relators, provided the weighted sum in the definition converges.

= Index-p rewriting bound for p-deficiency
{parent=p-deficiency}

If $H$ is a <normal subgroup> of index $p$ in a group presented by $\mathcal P$, the <Reidemeister–Schreier theorem> produces a presentation $\mathcal Q$ with
$$
\operatorname{def}_p(\mathcal Q)-1\geq p\bigl(\operatorname{def}_p(\mathcal P)-1\bigr).
$$
The preimage of $H$ in the <free group> has rank $1+p(d-1)$. A relator $w^{p^k}$ produces at most $p$ conjugate relators of weight $p^{-k}$ if its root lies in that preimage. If the root lies outside, $k\geq1$, and the coset conjugates are redundant up to conjugation; one relator of weight at most $p^{-(k-1)}$ suffices. Thus the total weighted relator cost grows by at most $p$. Convergent infinite sums are handled term by term.

= p-rank of a group
{parent=p-deficiency}
{title2=$d_p(G)$}

For a <finitely generated group>, let $G^p$ denote the subgroup generated by all $p$th powers. Its p-rank is
$$
d_p(G)=\dim_{\mathbb F_p}\bigl(G/[G,G]G^p\bigr).
$$
This is the dimension of the largest quotient that is an <elementary abelian group> of exponent $p$. In a <group presentation> with $d$ generators and $s$ relators that are not $p$th powers, reduction of the exponent sums modulo $p$ gives at most $s$ linear constraints. Hence $d_p(G)\geq d-s\geq\operatorname{def}_p(\mathcal P)$. In particular a presentation of p-deficiency at least one yields a surjection onto $C_p$.

= Word problem for groups
{parent=Group presentation}
{wiki}

= Word problem for a group
{synonym}

= Word problem
{synonym}

For a fixed finitely generated <group> with specified generators, its word problem asks for an algorithm deciding whether an input word represents the identity. For a finitely presented group, positive answers can be enumerated from the relators, but there need not be an algorithm deciding both positive and negative answers. An effective reduction from a nonrecursive <halting set> proves unsolvability.

= Soluble word problem in finite cyclic amalgams
{parent=Word problem for groups}

An <amalgamated free product> of finite cyclic groups has an effective <normal form theorem for an amalgamated free product>: multiplication, subgroup membership and subgroup identifications are finite table operations. Replacing an amalgamated syllable by its image in the neighbouring factor and combining adjacent syllables from one factor strictly decreases syllable count. A remaining alternating product outside the amalgamated subgroup is nonidentity. This supplies a terminating <word problem for a group> algorithm.

= Shift-invariant graph groups encode arbitrary sets
{parent=Word problem for groups}
{title2=$[b_0,t^nb_0t^{-n}]=1\ \Longleftrightarrow\ n\in S$}

For $S\subseteq\mathbb N_{>0}$, form the <right-angled Artin group> on generators $b_i$ indexed by $i\in\mathbb Z$, imposing $[b_i,b_j]=1$ precisely when $|i-j|\in S$. Translation $b_i\mapsto b_{i+1}$ is an automorphism. Its <semidirect product> with an infinite cyclic group generated by $t$ is countable and generated by $b_0,t$. If $n\notin S$, killing all $b_i$ except $b_0,b_n$ retracts the graph group onto the rank-two <free group>, so their commutator is nonidentity. This proves the displayed equivalence. For non-recursively-enumerable $S$, <enumerable identities of finitely generated subgroups> rules out an embedding of this two-generated group in any finitely presented group.

= Enumerable identities of finitely generated subgroups
{parent=Word problem for groups}

If a finitely generated <group> embeds in a <finitely presented group>, its null words in any fixed finite generating set form a <recursively enumerable set>. Choose words for the generator images in the larger group. Enumerate finite products of conjugates of its defining relators and their inverses, freely reduce, and compare with substituted words. Dovetailing over input words enumerates exactly the subgroup's identities. Choosing the finite image words is nonuniform, but their existence suffices for existence of an enumeration algorithm.

= Boone-Higman theorem
{c}
{parent=Word problem for groups}

= Algebraic characterization of soluble word problem
{synonym}

A finitely generated <group> $G$ has a soluble <word problem for a group> if and only if there are a <simple group> $S$ and a <finitely presented group> $P$ with embeddings $G\hookrightarrow S\hookrightarrow P$. The theorem does not require $S$ itself to be finitely presented. In particular, a finitely generated subgroup of a finitely presented simple group has soluble word problem. The primary statement is Theorem I of https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/journal-of-the-australian-mathematical-society/article/an-algebraic-characterization-of-groups-with-soluble-word-problem1/748FFD937CEA3727E812C8CDB9CC59B9[An algebraic characterization of groups with soluble word problem].

= Semidecision of nonidentity in simple subgroups
{parent=Boone-Higman theorem}

Let $S$ be a nontrivial <simple group> inside a <finitely presented group> $P$, and fix $a\ne1$ in $S$. For an input word $w$ in $S$, enumerate products of conjugates in $P$ of $w^{\pm1}$ and proofs in $P$ that such a product equals $a$. This search terminates exactly when $w\ne1$: the <normal closure> of a nontrivial element of $S$ is all of $S$, and its conjugators are also elements of $P$, while a null word has only trivial conjugates. Run this search alongside the enumerable proofs that $w=1$. Exactly one search terminates, solving the <word problem for a group> on any specified finitely generated subgroup of $S$. No effective generating set for the possibly infinitely generated $S$ is needed.

= Recursive simple envelope by cyclic HNN extensions
{parent=Boone-Higman theorem}

A <finitely generated group> with soluble <word problem for a group> embeds in a countably generated <simple group> with a <recursive presentation of a group>. Start with $P_0=G*\langle a\rangle$, retaining decidable membership in $\langle a\rangle$. At stage $n$, add a free infinite cyclic generator $z_n$. For every nonidentity base element $g$, conjugate $[g,z_n]$ to $a$ and conjugate $gz_n$ to $a$; also conjugate $z_n$ to $a$, using distinct <stable letters>. All associated subgroups are infinite cyclic, with decidable membership by <effective cyclic membership in a free product>. <Britton's lemma> preserves embeddings, decidable word problem and decidable membership in the inherited $\langle a\rangle$.

In the union, any nontrivial normal subgroup contains some nonidentity $g$ and hence $a$, by the commutator-conjugation relation. The other relations force $z_n$ and then every old $g$ into the normal closure of $a$. Every stable letter becomes an old element at a later stage, so the normal closure of $a$ is the whole union. The union is nontrivial and simple. Effective word enumeration at each stage makes all relators recursively enumerable. A <two-generator HNN embedding> followed by the <Higman embedding theorem> embeds this simple envelope in a finitely presented group.

= Finite group presentation
{parent=Group presentation}
{title2=$\langle X\mid R\rangle$}

A finite group presentation has finitely many generators and finitely many relators. It defines the quotient of the <free group> on $X$ by the <normal closure> of $R$. <Free products> of finitely presented groups and <HNN extensions> along explicitly finitely generated associated subgroups have finite presentations obtained by adjoining finitely many generators and relations.

= Finitely presented group
{parent=Finite group presentation}

A finitely presented group is a <group> admitting a <finite group presentation>. Finite presentability concerns the existence of such a presentation, not the decidability of its <word problem for a group>.

= Independence of finite presentation from finite generators
{parent=Finitely presented group}

Suppose $G=\langle Y\mid R(Y)\rangle$ is a finite <group presentation>, and $X$ is any finite generating set. Write $y_j=u_j(X)$ and $x_i=v_i(Y)$. A finite presentation on $X$ is obtained using the relators $R(u(X))$ and $x_i=v_i(u(X))$. The substitutions in both directions give inverse homomorphisms, proving that finite presentability does not depend on the finite generators chosen.

// Target: geometry-and-topology.bigb

= Higman group
{c}
{parent=Finitely presented group}
{title2=$Q$}
{wiki}

The Higman group has the <finite group presentation> $Q=\langle a_1,a_2,a_3,a_4\mid a_i a_{i+1}a_i^{-1}=a_{i+1}^2\ (i\bmod4)\rangle$. Its subgroup $\langle a_1,a_3\rangle$ is a rank-two <free group>, and the <normal closure> of each $a_i$ is all of $Q$. It has no nontrivial <finite quotients of a group>: a least prime divisor among the four generator orders in a finite image contradicts the multiplicative-order constraints imposed by conjugation to squares. These properties give finite-presentation constructions embedding arbitrary finitely presented groups into groups with no nontrivial finite quotients.

= Higman group as an amalgam of iterated HNN extensions
{c}
{parent=Higman group}
{title2=$\langle a,b,c,d\mid a^{-1}ba=b^2,\ b^{-1}cb=c^2,\ c^{-1}dc=d^2,\ d^{-1}ad=a^2\rangle$}

The two three-generator components on $\{a,b,c\}$ and $\{c,d,a\}$ are iterated <HNN extensions> of infinite cyclic groups. The subgroup on $\{a,c\}$ is free of rank two in each component by <Britton's lemma>. Amalgamating over these free subgroups gives the displayed infinite <finitely presented group>. Its cyclic squaring relations forbid every nontrivial finite quotient by the least-prime multiplicative-order argument.

= Finite quotients of cyclic squaring presentations
{parent=Higman group}

A <group presentation> with a cyclic list of generators $a_i$ and relations $a_i a_{i+1}a_i^{-1}=a_{i+1}^2$ has no nontrivial <finite quotient of a group>. In a finite image, all generator orders are odd. Choose the least prime $p$ dividing any nontrivial generator order and let $x$ be the predecessor of a generator $y$ whose order is divisible by $p$. Conjugation by $x$ acts as squaring on $\langle y\rangle$, so the <multiplicative order> of $2$ modulo $p$ divides the order of $x$. That multiplicative order is greater than one and divides $p-1$, giving a prime divisor smaller than $p$ in the order of $x$, a contradiction. This argument applies to any cycle length; it asserts absence of finite quotients, not infinitude of the presented group.

= Markov property of finitely presented groups
{c}
{parent=Finitely presented group}
{title2=$\rho$}

An isomorphism-invariant property of <finitely presented groups> is Markov when some finitely presented group has it and some finitely presented group cannot embed in any finitely presented group having it. The second witness is an obstruction to embedding, rather than merely a group failing the property. The <Adian–Rabin theorem> makes every such property algorithmically undecidable from arbitrary finite presentations.

= Adian–Rabin theorem
{c}
{parent=Markov property of finitely presented groups}
{wiki}

No <Markov property of finitely presented groups> is decidable by an algorithm taking an arbitrary <finite group presentation> as input. The proof reduces the <word problem for a group> to property recognition using an effective construction which collapses when an input word is trivial and embeds the input group when it is nontrivial.

= Messuage lemma for groups
{c}
{parent=Adian–Rabin theorem}

In the effective group form used for recognition problems, a <finite group presentation> of $K$ and a word $w$ yield, by an algorithm, a finite presentation of a group $K_w$. If $w=1$ in $K$, then $K_w$ is trivial; if $w\ne1$ in $K$, then the specified generators embed $K$ in $K_w$. One can additionally arrange that the <normal closure> of the image of $w$ is all of $K_w$. The finite presentation can be written without first deciding which case holds. This is the collapse-versus-embedding construction behind the <Adian–Rabin theorem>.

= Tietze transformations
{c}
{parent=Group presentation}
{wiki}

= Tietze transformation
{c}
{synonym}

A Tietze transformation changes a <group presentation> without changing the presented <group>, by adding or removing a consequence of the relators, or by adding or eliminating a generator together with a defining equation expressing it in the others. For example a generator $t$ defined by $t=xsx^{-1}$ can be eliminated by substitution in every remaining relator.

= Dehn presentation
{parent=Group presentation}
{c}

A finite <group presentation> with cyclically reduced symmetrized <relators> is a <Dehn presentation> if every nonempty freely reduced null word contains a <relator> segment longer than half that <relator>. Replacing the segment by the inverse complementary segment strictly shortens the word. Finiteness of both the alphabet and the <relator> set is part of this definition; merely permitting all null words as an infinite <relator> set would make the shortening property vacuous as a finiteness criterion.

= Torsion conjugacy bound for a Dehn presentation
{parent=Dehn presentation}
{title2=$|w|\leq\lfloor|r|/2\rfloor$}

In a finite <Dehn presentation>, every nonidentity finite-order element has a <shortest conjugacy representative> of length at most half the length of some <relator>. Indeed a positive power contains a Dehn segment. If the representative had length at least $k=\lfloor|r|/2\rfloor+1$, the first $k$ letters of that segment would fit into a cyclic rotation and could be replaced by fewer letters, contradicting minimality. Bounded <relator> lengths and a finite alphabet therefore give finitely many <conjugacy classes> of finite-order elements. With no <relators> the group is free and torsion-free, so only the identity class remains.

= Dehn algorithm
{parent=Dehn presentation}
{c}
{wiki}

For a <Dehn presentation>, repeatedly freely reduce a word or replace a <relator> segment longer than half its perimeter by its shorter complementary segment. Length decreases strictly. A word represents the identity exactly when the procedure reaches the empty word: any nonempty terminal null word would contradict the defining Dehn property. The finite list of <relators> makes each search effective.

= Symmetrized relator set
{parent=Group presentation}

A set of <relator> words is symmetrized if it includes their inverses and cyclic permutations. Symmetrizing a finite set remains finite, preserves each <relator>'s length and presents the same group. It permits a matching <relator> segment to be taken anywhere on either orientation of a cell boundary.

= Universal property of a group presentation
{parent=Group presentation}

A map from the generators $S$ of $\langle S\mid R\rangle$ to a group $G$ extends to a homomorphism exactly when every relator in $R$ evaluates to the identity. The extension is then unique.

= Relator
{parent=Group presentation}

A relator is a word in the generators of a <free group> that is imposed as the identity in a <group presentation>.

= Free presentation
{parent=Group presentation}

A free presentation of a group is a quotient $G\cong F/R$ in which $F$ is a <free group> and $R$ is the <normal closure> of the chosen <relators>.

= Balanced presentation
{parent=Free presentation}
{wiki}

A finite group presentation is balanced when it has the same number of generators and relators.

= Relator exponent-sum matrix
{parent=Balanced presentation}

For a finite <group presentation>, the relator exponent-sum matrix records in row $i$ the total exponent of each generator in relator $r_i$. It presents the <abelianization> of the group. For a balanced presentation of a <perfect group>, it is a <unimodular matrix>.

= Relation module
{title2=$R/[R,R]$}
{parent=Free presentation}

For a <free presentation> $G\cong F/R$, the relation module is the abelianization $R/[R,R]$ equipped with the $G$-action induced by conjugation in $F$.

= Presentation relation sequence
{parent=Relation module}

If $I_F$ is the augmentation ideal of $\mathbb ZF$, a <free presentation> $G\cong F/R$ gives an exact sequence
$$
0\longrightarrow R/[R,R]\longrightarrow\mathbb ZG\otimes_{\mathbb ZF}I_F
\longrightarrow\mathbb ZG\longrightarrow\mathbb Z\longrightarrow0.
$$
The middle module is free over $\mathbb ZG$ on the free generators of $F$.

= Fox calculus
{c}
{parent=Free presentation}
{wiki}

= Fox free differential calculus
{c}
{synonym}

Fox calculus assigns noncommutative derivatives $\partial/\partial x_i$ to words in a free group. The product rule and the identity
$$
r-1=\sum_i\frac{\partial r}{\partial x_i}(x_i-1)
$$
turn relators into module maps and give the presentation relation sequence.

= Fox derivative
{c}
{parent=Fox calculus}
{title2=$\partial/\partial x$}

The Fox derivative of a word in a <free group> obeys $\partial(uv)/\partial x=\partial u/\partial x+u\,\partial v/\partial x$ and $\partial x^{-1}/\partial x=-x^{-1}$.

= Area of a null-homotopic word
{title2=$\operatorname{Area}(w)$}
{parent=Group presentation}

For a word $w\in F(S)$ representing the identity in $\langle S\mid R\rangle$, its area is the least $N$ for which
$$
w=\prod_{i=1}^N u_i r_i^{\varepsilon_i}u_i^{-1}
$$
in $F(S)$, where $r_i\in R$ and $\varepsilon_i\in\{-1,1\}$.

= Dehn function
{title2=$\delta(n)$}
{c}
{parent=Area of a null-homotopic word}
{wiki}

The Dehn function of a finite presentation is
$$
\delta(n)=\max\{\operatorname{Area}(w):w=1,\ |w|\leq n\}.
$$
It measures the worst number of relator applications needed to prove a null word of bounded length.

= Dehn functions under a change of finite presentation
{parent=Dehn function}

Choose bounded-length substitutions $T$ from the first generating set to the second, and $U$ back. Each substituted <relator> of the second <group presentation> has bounded area in the first, and every generator correction $a(UT(a))^{-1}$ has bounded area. Substitution into a minimal second-presentation diagram and filling the correction strips gives
$$
\delta_1(n)\le C\delta_2(Ln)+Dn.
$$
The reverse construction gives the opposite inequality. Thus the optimal <Dehn functions> are coarsely equivalent, and the existence of a recursive isoperimetric bound, hence solvability of the <word problem for a group>, is independent of the <finite group presentation>.

= Isoperimetric function of a group presentation
{parent=Dehn function}

= Isoperimetric function
{synonym}

A nondecreasing function bounding the area of every null word of length at most $n$ is an <isoperimetric function> for a <finite group presentation>. The <Dehn function> is the minimal such bound. A subrecursive bound means one dominated by a total computable function. Arbitrarily enlarged bounds are not invariants: even a <group presentation> with zero area function admits both linear and exponential upper bounds, which are not equivalent under the usual coarse comparison.

= Recursive area bounds and the word problem
{parent=Isoperimetric function of a group presentation}

A <finite group presentation> has a recursive upper bound on its <Dehn function> exactly when its <word problem for a group> is decidable. If the maximal <relator> length is $\ell$ and a word has length $n$, a diagram of area at most $N$ can be chosen with at most $\ell N+n$ edges. Enumerating all finite labelled planar diagrams within that bound gives a terminating test. Conversely, a word decider identifies the finitely many null words of length at most $n$; exhaustive finite diagram searches find their minimal areas, whose maximum computes the <Dehn function>.

= Cayley graph
{c}
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

For a group $G$ with generating set $S$, the Cayley graph $\operatorname{Cay}_S(G)$ has vertex set $G$ and an edge from $g$ to $gs$ for each $s\in S$.

= Schreier graph
{c}
{parent=Cayley graph}
{wiki}

For a <group> $G$, a <subgroup> $H$, and a generating set $S$, the Schreier graph has the left cosets $G/H$ as vertices and an edge from $gH$ to $sgH$ for each $s\in S$. Its radius-$m$ ball records the cosets met by $S^m$.

= Diameter of a finite group with respect to a generating set
{title2=$\operatorname{diam}_S(G)$}
{parent=Cayley graph}

For a <finite group> $G$ with a symmetric <generating set> $S$, its diameter is
$$
\operatorname{diam}_S(G)=\min\{n:S^n=G\}.
$$
Equivalently, it is the diameter of the <Cayley graph> $\operatorname{Cay}_S(G)$.

= Trivial diameter bound for a finite group
{parent=Diameter of a finite group with respect to a generating set}

A connected <Cayley graph> on a finite group $G$ has $|G|$ vertices, and a shortest path repeats no vertex. Therefore
$$
\operatorname{diam}_S(G)\leq|G|-1.
$$

= Diameter bound for a non-abelian finite simple group
{parent=Diameter of a finite group with respect to a generating set}

For every $\varepsilon>0$ there is $\lambda(\varepsilon)$ such that every non-abelian finite <simple group> $G$ and every finite symmetric generating set $S$ containing the identity satisfy
$$
\operatorname{diam}_S(G)\leq\max\{|G|^\varepsilon,\lambda(\varepsilon)\}.
$$
The proof applies the <one-scale virtual nilpotence theorem> below the diameter and then uses simplicity to eliminate both the bounded-index subgroup and the finite normal subgroup.

= Word metric
{title2=$d_S$}
{parent=Cayley graph}
{wiki}

The word metric associated with a generating set $S$ is the path metric on $\operatorname{Cay}_S(G)$, equivalently $d_S(g,h)=|g^{-1}h|_S$.

= Word length
{parent=Word metric}
{title2=$|g|_S$}

The <word length> $|g|_S$ is the least number of letters from a symmetric <generating set of a group> $S$ whose product is $g$. The empty word has length zero. It obeys $|gh|_S\leq|g|_S+|h|_S$, $|g^{-1}|_S=|g|_S$ and $d_S(g,h)=|g^{-1}h|_S$. The generating set may be infinite; finiteness is needed only for additional conclusions such as finite metric balls.

= Stable word length
{parent=Word metric}
{title2=$\tau_S(g)$}

= Stable translation length in a group
{synonym}

For a <word metric> from a finite generating set, the <stable word length> is
$$
\tau_S(g)=\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{|g^n|_S}{n}=\inf_{n\geq1}\frac{|g^n|_S}{n}.
$$
Subadditivity and the <Fekete lemma> give the limit. Conjugating $g^n$ changes its length by at most a constant, so <stable word length> is conjugacy invariant. It satisfies $\tau_S(g^k)=|k|\tau_S(g)$. Finite-order elements have zero <stable word length>; in a <hyperbolic group>, every infinite-order element has positive <stable word length>.

= Equivalence of finite word metrics
{parent=Word metric}

For two finite symmetric choices of <generating set of a group> $S,T$, let $K=\max(\{1\}\cup\{|s|_T:s\in S\})$ and $L=\max(\{1\}\cup\{|t|_S:t\in T\})$. Replacing letters by their fixed words gives $|g|_T\leq K|g|_S$ and $|g|_S\leq L|g|_T$. Thus the identity is a <bilipschitz equivalence> between the <word metrics>. Finiteness is essential for these maxima; an infinite generating set can collapse whole subgroups to diameter one.

= Infinite line as a Cayley graph
{parent=Cayley graph}

The undirected infinite line is both the <Cayley graph> of the <infinite cyclic group> $\mathbb Z$ with generating set $\{1\}$ and the Cayley graph of the <infinite dihedral group> $C_2*C_2$ with its two involutory generators. Thus an unlabelled Cayley graph need not determine its group.

= Milnor–Švarc lemma
{c}
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

If a <group> has a <properly discontinuous group action> by <isometries> that is also a <cocompact group action> on a nonempty <proper metric space> that is a <geodesic metric space>, then the <group> is finitely generated and every <orbit map> from the <word metric> for any finite <generating set of a group> is a <quasi-isometry>. Finite stabilizers are permitted.

= Cobounded orbit map lemma
{parent=Milnor–Švarc lemma}

A <cobounded group action> by isometries on a nonempty <geodesic metric space> admits an <orbit map> that is a <quasi-isometry> for a possibly infinite <generating set of a group>. If $Gx$ is $R$-dense, take $S=\{g\ne1:d(x,gx)\leq2R+1\}$. Subdivide a <metric geodesic> into segments of length at most one and approximate subdivision vertices by orbit points, keeping the endpoints exact. The resulting group increments generate and give
$$
|g|_S\leq d(x,gx)+1,\qquad d(x,gx)\leq(2R+1)|g|_S.
$$
The first estimate includes zero displacement: a nontrivial stabilizer element has length one. No finiteness assertion about $S$ survives without properness.

= Covering graph
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

A covering graph is a graph map that induces a bijection on the incident oriented edges at every vertex. Connected based covers of a graph correspond to subgroups of its fundamental group.

= Schreier coset graph
{c}
{parent=Covering graph}
{wiki}

For $H\leq G$ and a generating set $S$, the Schreier coset graph has vertices $Hg$ and a directed $s$-edge from $Hg$ to $Hgs$ for every $s\in S$. For a free group, this is the covering graph associated with $H$.

= Core graph of a subgroup
{parent=Covering graph}

The core of a based covering graph is the union of all reduced loops through its base vertex. A subgroup of a finitely generated free group is finitely generated exactly when its core graph is finite.

= Bass-Serre theory
{c}
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki=Bass–Serre_theory}

Bass-Serre theory describes group splittings as actions on trees and reconstructs a graph of groups from the stabilizers of such an action.

= Bass-Serre tree
{title2=$T$}
{c}
{parent=Bass-Serre theory}

For $G=A*_CB$, the Bass-Serre tree has vertices $G/A\sqcup G/B$ and edges $G/C$, with $gC$ joining $gA$ to $gB$. Vertex degrees are the indices of the corresponding images of $C$.

= HNN extension
{c}
{parent=Bass-Serre theory}
{wiki}

Given isomorphic subgroups $C,D\leq A$ and an isomorphism $\phi:C\to D$, the HNN extension is
$$
\langle A,t\mid tct^{-1}=\phi(c)\text{ for every }c\in C\rangle.
$$
Its stable letter $t$ records the loop edge in the associated graph of groups.

= Torsion in an HNN extension
{parent=HNN extension}

Every finite-order element of an <HNN extension> is conjugate to a finite-order element of its base. Choose a conjugate minimizing the number of <stable letters>. If this number is positive, no pinch is possible either internally or across the end-to-start join, since a boundary pinch would allow a conjugation shortening it. Every positive power is then reduced with positive stable-letter length, and <Britton's lemma> makes it nonidentity. In particular, an HNN extension of a <torsion-free group> is torsion-free.

= Centralizing HNN extension detects subgroup membership
{parent=HNN extension}
{title2=$t^{-1}ptp^{-1}=1\ \Longleftrightarrow\ p\in L$}

In the <HNN extension> $\langle P,t\mid t^{-1}\ell t=\ell\ (\ell\in L)\rangle$, the displayed equivalence follows from <Britton's lemma>. If $p\in L$, the word collapses by the defining isomorphism; otherwise it has stable letters and no pinch, so it is nonidentity. When $P$ is finitely presented and $L$ is finitely generated, imposing commutation with the finitely many subgroup generators gives a finite presentation. An undecidable membership problem for $L$ then becomes an undecidable <word problem for a group> in the extension.

= Finite presentation of a centralizing HNN extension detects finite generation of its associated subgroup
{parent=Centralizing HNN extension detects subgroup membership}

For a finitely presented base group $G$ and subgroup $H$, the extension $\langle G,t\mid[t,h]=1\ (h\in H)\rangle$ is finitely presented exactly when $H$ is finitely generated. Sufficiency follows by imposing commutation with finitely many subgroup generators. For necessity, any finite set normally generating the defining kernel uses only finitely many centralizing relators. If their subgroup $H_0$ is proper in $H$, <Britton's lemma> makes $t^{-1}ht h^{-1}$ nonidentity in the partial extension for $h\in H\setminus H_0$, contradicting the claimed kernel equality.

// Target: algebra.bigb

= Adjoining a root by an HNN extension
{parent=HNN extension}
{title2=$h^n=g$}

To adjoin an $n$th root of $g\in G$, let $m$ be the <order of a group element> $g$ and take a <cyclic group> $C=\langle a\rangle$ of order $nm$ when $m$ is finite, or infinite order when $g$ has infinite order. In the <free product> $G*C$, $\langle a^n\rangle$ and $\langle g\rangle$ have the same order. The <HNN extension> with $t^{-1}a^nt=g$ therefore embeds its base by <Britton's lemma>, and $h=t^{-1}at$ satisfies $h^n=g$. If $g=1$, take $C$ of order $n$ and identify the two trivial subgroups. This is an existence construction, not an algorithm for finding an unknown element order.

= Normal form theorem for an HNN extension
{parent=HNN extension}

Fix <right coset transversals> $R_+,R_-$ for $A,B$, containing $1$. In the convention $tat^{-1}=\phi(a)$, every element has a unique normal form $g_0t^{\epsilon_1}r_1\cdots t^{\epsilon_k}r_k$, with $r_i\in R_{\epsilon_i}$ and $r_i\ne1$ whenever $\epsilon_{i+1}=-\epsilon_i$. A permutation action on the set of these formal sequences proves uniqueness and embeds the base <group>. Transporting subgroup coefficients across stable letters converts general words to these normal forms.

= Permutation proof of HNN normal form uniqueness
{parent=Normal form theorem for an HNN extension}

With convention $t^{-1}at=\phi(a)$, choose representatives of the right cosets of $A$ and $B$ in the base group, with identity representing each subgroup. Normal strings have the form $g_0t^{\epsilon_1}r_1\cdots t^{\epsilon_n}r_n$, where $r_i$ represents a coset of $B$ for $\epsilon_i=1$ and of $A$ for $\epsilon_i=-1$, and $r_i\ne1$ when adjacent exponents are opposite. Left multiplication by base elements or stable letters defines permutations of these strings. They satisfy the HNN relations. Applying the induced action to the empty string distinguishes different normal strings, proving uniqueness without presupposing <Britton's lemma>.

// Target: geometry-and-topology.bigb

= Reduced sequence in an HNN extension
{parent=Normal form theorem for an HNN extension}

= Reduced sequences in an HNN extension
{synonym}

A reduced sequence is a word $g_0t^{\epsilon_1}g_1\cdots t^{\epsilon_k}g_k$ with no <pinch in an HNN extension>. If $k>0$, <Britton's lemma> makes it nontrivial and prevents it from representing a base-group element. Reduced sequences need not be unique; uniqueness requires the fixed coset representatives in the <normal form theorem for an HNN extension>.

= Cyclically reduced sequence in an HNN extension
{parent=Reduced sequence in an HNN extension}

A cyclically reduced sequence has no <pinch in an HNN extension> either internally or across its cyclic junction. If its stable-letter length is positive, every positive power remains reduced, so <Britton's lemma> gives infinite order. Repeated cyclic conjugation and pinch removal shows that any finite-order element of an <HNN extension> is conjugate into its base <group>.

= Pinch in an HNN extension
{parent=Normal form theorem for an HNN extension}
{title2=$tat^{-1},\ t^{-1}bt$}

= Pinches in an HNN extension
{synonym}

In $\langle G,t\mid tat^{-1}=\phi(a),\ a\in A\rangle$, a pinch is a subword $tat^{-1}$ with $a\in A$, or $t^{-1}bt$ with $b\in B=\phi(A)$. It can be replaced by a base-group element, removing two stable letters. <Britton's lemma> asserts that every identity word with stable letters must have a pinch after base-group multiplication is performed.

= Associated subgroup of an HNN extension
{parent=HNN extension}
{title2=$A,B$}

= Associated subgroups of an HNN extension
{synonym}

The associated subgroups are the source and target $A,B\le G$ of the <isomorphism> used to define an <HNN extension>. They determine its <pinches in an HNN extension>, its coset transversals, and the possible cancellations of adjacent inverse <stable letters>.

= Stable letter
{parent=HNN extension}
{title2=$t$}

A stable letter is the new generator of an <HNN extension> which implements an <isomorphism> between two specified subgroups by conjugation. In the convention $tat^{-1}=\phi(a)$, it moves elements of the source subgroup $A$ to the target subgroup $B$; using $t^{-1}at=\phi(a)$ reverses the naming convention.

= Britton's lemma
{c}
{parent=HNN extension}
{wiki}

= Britton lemma
{c}
{synonym}

In an HNN extension, every word containing a stable letter and having no pinch $t^{-1}ct$ or $tdt^{-1}$ is nonidentity. In particular, the natural map from the base group to its HNN extension is injective.

= Baumslag-Solitar group
{title2=$\operatorname{BS}(m,n)$}
{c}
{parent=HNN extension}
{wiki=Baumslag–Solitar_group}

The Baumslag-Solitar group is
$$
\operatorname{BS}(m,n)=\langle a,t\mid ta^mt^{-1}=a^n\rangle.
$$

= Exponential growth of the ternary affine Baumslag-Solitar group
{parent=Baumslag-Solitar group}
{title2=$\langle x,y\mid x^{-1}yx=y^3\rangle$}

Represent $y$ by translation $t\mapsto t+1$ and $x$ by $t\mapsto t/3$; the <relator> holds and $y$ has infinite order. Base-three digits $d_j\in\{0,1,2\}$ give words recursively by $W_0=1$, $W_{j+1}=x^{-1}W_jxy^{d_j}$. These have length at most $4k$ and represent all $y^m$, $0\le m<3^k$. Thus the <growth function of a finitely generated group> obeys $\beta(4k)\ge3^k$. Counting freely reduced words in four letters gives $\beta(n)\le2\cdot3^n-1$. The growth is therefore exponential up to coarse equivalence.

= Iterated endomorphism kernels in BS(2,3)
{parent=Baumslag-Solitar group}
{title2=$G''\subseteq\bigcup_{k\ge1}\ker\theta^k\subseteq R_G$}

For $G=\langle a,t\mid ta^2t^{-1}=a^3\rangle$, the surjective endomorphism $\theta(a)=a^2$, $\theta(t)=t$ has nontrivial kernel by <Britton's lemma>. The <commutator subgroup> is generated by $a_i=t^iat^{-i}$ for $i\in\mathbb Z$. For $j>i$, its defining relations give $a_j^{2^{j-i}}=a_i^{3^{j-i}}$. Hence $\theta^k([a_i,a_j])=1$ whenever $k\ge j-i$. The union of the increasing kernels is normal, and the images of all $a_i$ commute in its quotient, proving the first inclusion. The second follows from <finite residual contains surjective endomorphism kernels> applied to every iterate.

= Dyadic affine Baumslag-Solitar group
{parent=Baumslag-Solitar group}
{title2=$\operatorname{BS}(1,2)\cong\mathbb Z[1/2]\rtimes\mathbb Z$}

In the <finite group presentation> $\langle a,t\mid tat^{-1}=a^2\rangle$, let $a$ act by $x\mapsto x+1$ and $t$ by $x\mapsto2x$. The conjugates $a_i=t^{-i}at^i$ satisfy $a_{i+1}^2=a_i$, so their increasing cyclic subgroups give the additive group $\mathbb Z[1/2]$. Every element has the form $a_i^m t^k$, and the affine action proves that this form can be the identity only if $k=m=0$. Thus the displayed <semidirect product> identification holds. This is a finitely presented group with a series having torsion-free abelian factors, but it is not a <poly-infinite cyclic group>, since $\mathbb Z[1/2]$ is not finitely generated.

= Normal form theorem for an amalgamated free product
{parent=Bass-Serre theory}

In $A*_CB$, an alternating product of elements of $A\setminus C$ and $B\setminus C$ is nonidentity. More generally, choosing coset representatives gives a unique reduced normal form.

This is the amalgamated case of <normal form for free groups and free product of groups>.

= Center of a proper amalgam lies in the amalgamated subgroup
{parent=Normal form theorem for an amalgamated free product}

If C is proper in both factors of $G=G_1*_CG_2$, every central element lies in C. In the <Bass-Serre tree>, a central element fixing a vertex fixes its entire orbit and then the whole tree, hence an edge. A central element acting as a translation would have an invariant axis; minimality forces the tree to be that axis, but a vertex stabilizer then contains a reflection, contradicting centrality of the translation. Thus the center fixes an edge and is contained in its stabilizer C.

// Target: algebraic-topology.bigb

= Klein bottle group
{c}
{parent=Bass-Serre theory}

The fundamental group of the Klein bottle has presentation
$$
\langle a,b\mid aba^{-1}=b^{-1}\rangle.
$$
Its subgroup $\langle a^2,b\rangle\cong\mathbb Z^2$ has index two.

= Group action on a tree
{parent=Geometric group theory}

A group action on a tree is a homomorphism to the tree's combinatorial isometry group. Barycentric subdivision removes edge inversions without changing the essential action.

= Free group action on a tree
{parent=Group action on a tree}

A group acts freely on a tree when only the identity fixes a vertex. The quotient is a graph, the tree is its universal cover, and choosing lifts of a maximal subtree identifies the acting group with the fundamental group of the quotient graph. It is therefore a <free group>.

= Isometry of a tree
{parent=Group action on a tree}

Every inversion-free tree isometry is either elliptic, fixing a vertex, or hyperbolic, translating along a unique bi-infinite geodesic.

= Elliptic isometry of a tree
{parent=Isometry of a tree}

An elliptic tree isometry fixes a vertex. Its fixed-point set is a nonempty convex subtree.

= Hyperbolic isometry of a tree
{parent=Isometry of a tree}

A hyperbolic tree isometry has positive translation length and preserves a unique bi-infinite geodesic.

= Axis of a tree isometry
{title2=$A_g$}
{parent=Hyperbolic isometry of a tree}

The axis of a hyperbolic tree isometry $g$ is its unique invariant line. The isometry acts on this line by translation through its translation length.

= Translation length
{title2=$\ell(g)$}
{parent=Isometry of a tree}
{wiki}

The translation length of an isometry $g$ of a metric space is $\ell(g)=\inf_x d(x,gx)$. For a hyperbolic tree isometry, it is attained exactly on the <axis of a tree isometry>.

= Serre lemma for tree actions
{c}
{parent=Group action on a tree}

If two elliptic isometries $g,h$ of a tree have elliptic product $gh$, then their fixed subtrees intersect.

= Helly property
{c}
{parent=Group action on a tree}

Finite families of convex subtrees of a tree have the Helly property: if every pair intersects, then their total intersection is nonempty.

In the terminology of a <Helly family>, convex subtrees form a family of Helly order two.

= Fuchsian group
{c}
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

A Fuchsian group is a discrete subgroup of $\operatorname{PSL}_2(\mathbb R)$, acting by orientation-preserving isometries of the hyperbolic plane. It is non-elementary when it has no finite orbit in the hyperbolic plane or its ideal boundary.

= Parabolic isometry of the hyperbolic plane
{parent=Fuchsian group}

A nonidentity orientation-preserving isometry of the <hyperbolic plane> is parabolic when it has one fixed point on the ideal boundary and none in the plane. In the <upper half-plane model>, every parabolic isometry is conjugate to a nonzero horizontal translation.

This is one type of <isometry> of the <hyperbolic plane>, classified by its fixed points.

= Hyperbolic triangle group
{title2=$\Delta(p,q,r)$}
{parent=Fuchsian group}

When $1/p+1/q+1/r<1$, the orientation-preserving hyperbolic triangle group has presentation
$$
\Delta(p,q,r)=\langle a,b,c\mid a^p=b^q=c^r=abc=1\rangle
$$
and is a non-elementary Fuchsian group.

This is the orientation-preserving hyperbolic member of the broader <triangle group> construction.

= Quasi-isometry
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

A map $f:X\to Y$ is a $(\lambda,\varepsilon)$-quasi-isometric embedding when
$$
\lambda^{-1}d_X(x,x')-\varepsilon
\leq d_Y(f(x),f(x'))
\leq\lambda d_X(x,x')+\varepsilon.
$$
It is a quasi-isometry when every point of $Y$ lies a uniformly bounded distance from its image.

= Quasi-inverse of a quasi-isometry
{parent=Quasi-isometry}

For a <quasi-isometry> $f:X\to Y$ with multiplicative constant $\lambda$ and additive/coarse-surjectivity constant $\epsilon$, choose $f'(y)$ with $d_Y(f(f'(y)),y)\le\epsilon$. The triangle inequality and the two distortion inequalities give
$$
\lambda^{-1}d_Y(y,y')-3\epsilon/\lambda\le d_X(f'(y),f'(y'))\le\lambda d_Y(y,y')+3\lambda\epsilon.
$$
Also $d_X(f'(f(x)),x)\le2\lambda\epsilon$. Hence $f'$ is a <quasi-isometry> and both composites stay uniformly close to their respective identities. No continuity is required.

= Finite-index subgroup quasi-isometry
{parent=Quasi-isometry}

If $H$ is a finite-index subgroup of a finitely generated group $G$, then $H$ is finitely generated and its inclusion into $G$ is a <quasi-isometry>. Finite generation follows from <Schreier's lemma>, while finitely many coset representatives give coarse surjectivity and uniformly bounded distortion.

= Schreier's lemma
{c}
{parent=Finite-index subgroup quasi-isometry}
{wiki}

If a group $G$ is generated by $S$, a subgroup $H$ has a set $T$ of right-coset representatives, and $\overline{ts}$ denotes the representative of $Hts$, then $H$ is generated by the Schreier generators
$$
ts\overline{ts}^{-1}
\qquad(t\in T, s\in S).
$$
In particular, every finite-index subgroup of a finitely generated group is finitely generated.

= Reidemeister–Schreier theorem
{c}
{parent=Schreier's lemma}
{wiki}

= Schreier lemma
{c}
{synonym}

Given a presentation of a group and a Schreier transversal for a subgroup, the Reidemeister–Schreier theorem gives a presentation of the subgroup. Its generators are the Schreier generators $ts\overline{ts}^{-1}$, and its relators are rewritten conjugates of the original relators.

= Schreier generator
{parent=Reidemeister–Schreier theorem}
{c}
{title2=$t g\,\overline{t g}^{-1}$}

For a subgroup $H$ of a group generated by a set $S$, choose representatives $t$ of right cosets $Ht$. If $\overline{tg}$ represents $Htg$, then $tg\,\overline{tg}^{-1}$ lies in $H$. These <Schreier generators> generate $H$: inserting successive coset representatives along a word telescopes the word into such factors. Lifted relators can be rewritten in the same factors.

= Finite-kernel quotient quasi-isometry
{parent=Quasi-isometry}

If a finitely generated group $G$ maps surjectively to $H$ with finite kernel, the quotient map is a <quasi-isometry>. After lifting a finite generating set of $H$, the only possible extra length lies in the finite kernel and is therefore uniformly bounded.

= Quasi-isometry criterion for a group homomorphism
{parent=Quasi-isometry}

For <finitely generated groups> $G,H$, a <group homomorphism> $\phi:G\to H$ is a <quasi-isometry> exactly when its <kernel> is finite and its image is a <finite-index subgroup> of $H$. The finite kernel controls collapse of distances, and finite index is exactly the coarse-surjectivity condition.

= Virtually free group
{parent=Quasi-isometry}
{wiki}

A group is virtually free when it has a <free group> as a finite-index subgroup. Every finitely generated virtually free group is quasi-isometric to a tree.

= Quasi-isometric embedding
{parent=Quasi-isometry}

A quasi-isometric embedding satisfies the two-sided coarse distance inequality in the definition of <quasi-isometry>, without requiring its image to be coarsely dense.

= Quasi-isometrically embedded subgroup
{parent=Quasi-isometry}

A finitely generated subgroup $H\leq G$ is quasi-isometrically embedded when its inclusion, equipped with word metrics from finite generating sets, is a <quasi-isometric embedding>. This property is independent of those generating sets.

= Retract subgroup
{parent=Quasi-isometrically embedded subgroup}

A subgroup $H\leq G$ is a retract when there is a <group homomorphism> $r:G\to H$ whose restriction to $H$ is the identity. If both groups are finitely generated, the inclusion of $H$ is a <quasi-isometric embedding>: the inclusion and retraction are both Lipschitz in their word metrics.

= Subgroup distortion
{parent=Quasi-isometrically embedded subgroup}

For a finitely generated subgroup $H\leq G$, subgroup distortion compares the intrinsic word length $|h|_H$ with the ambient word length $|h|_G$. The subgroup is undistorted exactly when its inclusion is a <quasi-isometric embedding>.

This is the subgroup word-metric instance of <distortion (mathematics)>.

= Quadratic distortion of the Heisenberg center
{parent=Subgroup distortion}
{title2=$|c^m|_H\asymp\sqrt{|m|}$}

The central infinite cyclic subgroup of the <integer Heisenberg group> has quadratic <subgroup distortion>. The identity $c^{k^2}=[a^k,b^k]$ gives ambient length at most $4k$ against intrinsic length $k^2$. For general $m\geq0$, take $k=\lfloor\sqrt m\rfloor$ and write $m=k^2+r$, $0\leq r\leq2k$, to obtain length at most $4k+r\leq6\sqrt m$. Conversely, a word of length $n$ in $a,b,c$ changes the first coordinate by at most $n$ and its central coordinate by at most $n^2+n$, so $|c^m|$ is bounded below by a positive multiple of $\sqrt{|m|}$ for $m\ne0$. Negative exponents follow by inverses. Thus the ambient and intrinsic metrics have quadratic relative growth.

= Exponentially distorted subgroup
{parent=Subgroup distortion}

A subgroup is exponentially distorted when elements with intrinsic length exponential in $n$ can have ambient length linear in $n$. In $\operatorname{BS}(1,2)$, the identity $b^nab^{-n}=a^{2^n}$ makes the cyclic subgroup generated by $a$ exponentially distorted.

= Bilipschitz equivalence
{parent=Quasi-isometry}

= Bilipschitz equivalent
{synonym}

A bilipschitz equivalence distorts all distances by multiplicative constants bounded away from zero and infinity. Every bilipschitz equivalence is a quasi-isometry.

= Quasigeodesic
{parent=Quasi-isometry}
{wiki}

A quasigeodesic is a quasi-isometric embedding of an interval into a metric space.

= Morse lemma for quasi-geodesics
{c}
{parent=Quasigeodesic}

In a Gromov-hyperbolic geodesic metric space, every quasigeodesic segment stays within a uniformly bounded Hausdorff distance of a geodesic segment with the same endpoints. The bound depends only on the hyperbolicity and quasigeodesic constants.

The bounded-distance conclusion is a property of a <Gromov-hyperbolic metric space>, under the geodesic and quasigeodesic hypotheses stated here.

= Hyperbolic metric space
{title2=$\delta$}
{parent=Quasi-isometry}
{wiki}

= Gromov-hyperbolic metric space
{c}
{synonym}

For $\delta\geq0$, a <geodesic metric space> is $\delta$-hyperbolic when each side of every <metric geodesic triangle> lies in the closed $\delta$-neighbourhood of the other two sides.

= Hyperbolicity is invariant under quasi-isometry
{parent=Hyperbolic metric space}

For <geodesic metric spaces>, a <quasi-isometry> preserves the property of being a <Gromov-hyperbolic metric space>. Images of <metric geodesic> sides are uniform <quasigeodesics>. The <Morse lemma for quasi-geodesics> keeps them uniformly close to <metric geodesic> sides in the hyperbolic target. Thinness there and the coarse lower distance bound pull a uniform triangle-thinness constant back to the source. Coarse surjectivity supplies a quasi-inverse for the reverse implication. Consequently a <metric geodesic> space quasi-isometric to a tree is hyperbolic, with no local finiteness assumption on the tree.

= Thin geodesic triangle
{parent=Hyperbolic metric space}

A geodesic triangle is $\delta$-thin when every point on each side lies within distance $\delta$ of one of the other two sides.

= Geodesic quadrilateral in a hyperbolic metric space
{parent=Hyperbolic metric space}

Every side of a geodesic quadrilateral in a $\delta$-hyperbolic metric space lies in the closed $2\delta$-neighbourhood of the other three sides. Indeed, a diagonal divides the quadrilateral into two $\delta$-thin triangles.

= Convex subset of a geodesic metric space
{parent=Hyperbolic metric space}

A subset $Y$ of a <geodesic metric space> is convex when every <metric geodesic> whose endpoints lie in $Y$ is contained in $Y$.

= Coarse uniqueness of a closest point in a hyperbolic metric space
{parent=Convex subset of a geodesic metric space}

If $Y$ is convex in a $\delta$-hyperbolic metric space and two points of $Y$ both minimize distance from $x$, then their distance is at most $4\delta$. Thus a closest-point projection to $Y$, when it exists, is unique up to uniformly bounded error.

= Closest point on a geodesic line in a hyperbolic metric space
{parent=Coarse uniqueness of a closest point in a hyperbolic metric space}

If $\gamma:\mathbb R\to X$ is an isometrically embedded geodesic line, then every $x\in X$ has a closest point on $\gamma$. The function $t\mapsto d(x,\gamma(t))$ is continuous and tends to infinity with $|t|$. Any two minimizing parameters differ by at most $4\delta$, and therefore by at most $6\delta$, in a $\delta$-hyperbolic metric space.

= Tripod points of a geodesic triangle
{parent=Hyperbolic metric space}

For a geodesic triangle with side lengths $a,b,c$, its tripod points divide the three sides into lengths
$$
\frac{a+b-c}{2},\qquad
\frac{b+c-a}{2},\qquad
\frac{c+a-b}{2}.
$$
In a $\delta$-hyperbolic metric space the three tripod points have pairwise distance at most $4\delta$.

= Hyperbolic group
{parent=Hyperbolic metric space}
{wiki}

A finitely generated group is hyperbolic when one, equivalently every, Cayley graph for a finite generating set is a <Gromov-hyperbolic metric space>.

= Infinite cyclic subgroups of hyperbolic groups are undistorted
{parent=Hyperbolic group}

If $g$ has infinite order in a <hyperbolic group>, the map $n\mapsto g^n$ is a <quasi-isometric embedding> of the integers. Equivalently $|g^n|\geq c|n|-C$ for some $c>0$. This is the homogeneous quasigeodesic theorem: local-to-global control of <metric geodesics> in a hyperbolic <Cayley graph> gives a quasigeodesic orbit for a suitable conjugate of a positive power; undoing <conjugation> and passing between consecutive powers changes only uniform constants. It implies $\tau(g)>0$. The theorem and its hypotheses are given in \a[https://loeh.app.uni-regensburg.de/teaching/ggt_ss22/lecture_notes.pdf][Löh, Theorem 6.5.9]. It is not true for arbitrary finitely generated groups, as the <Baumslag-Solitar group> relation $ab a^{-1}=b^3$ shows.

= Quasi-tree
{parent=Hyperbolic metric space}

A quasi-tree is a geodesic metric space quasi-isometric to a tree.

= Bottleneck property
{parent=Quasi-tree}

A geodesic metric space has the bottleneck property if every path joining the endpoints of a geodesic passes within one uniform distance of that geodesic's midpoint. A geodesic metric space is a quasi-tree exactly when it has this property.

= Quasiconvex subset
{parent=Geometric group theory}

A subset $Y$ of a <geodesic metric space> is $K$-quasiconvex when every <metric geodesic> between points of $Y$ lies in the closed $K$-neighborhood of $Y$.

= Quasiconvexity from no missing shells
{parent=Quasiconvex subset}
{title2=$C=L/2$}

Let $X$ be a finite combinatorial <2-complex> satisfying $C'(1/6)$, and let $Y$ be a connected <CW subcomplex> of its <universal cover> with no missing shells. If the largest cell perimeter is $L$, then $Y^{(1)}$ is $(L/2)$-quasiconvex in the universal cover's unit-edge <1-skeleton>. Bound an ambient <metric geodesic> and an intrinsic <metric geodesic> of $Y^{(1)}$ by a <reduced disc diagram>. A shell on the ambient side would shorten a <metric geodesic>. A shell on the intrinsic side belongs to $Y$ and would shorten its intrinsic <metric geodesic>. Spurs are likewise excluded from side interiors. The <Greendlinger ladder theorem> forces a ladder between the two marked corners. Each cell gives a path of length at most half its perimeter from either side to the other.

= Quasiconvex subgroup
{parent=Quasiconvex subset}
{wiki}

A subgroup is quasiconvex when it is a <quasiconvex subset> of a Cayley graph. In a <hyperbolic group>, a finitely generated subgroup is quasiconvex exactly when it is <quasi-isometrically embedded subgroup>[quasi-isometrically embedded].

= Displacement function
{title2=$d_g(x)$}
{parent=Geometric group theory}

For an isometry $g$ of a metric space, its displacement function is $d_g(x)=d(x,gx)$. It is $2$-Lipschitz.

= Proper metric space
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

A metric space is proper when every closed bounded subset is compact.

= Cocompact group action
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

A group action on a space is cocompact when its quotient is compact, equivalently when some compact set has translates covering the space.

= Properly discontinuous group action
{parent=Geometric group theory}

= Properly discontinuous
{synonym}

= Properly discontinuously
{synonym}

An action is properly discontinuous when every compact set meets only finitely many of its translates. For an isometric action on a proper metric space, this gives the finiteness needed to extract a constant group element from a sequence with bounded source and target.

For a discrete <group action> on a locally compact space, a standard proper-discontinuity condition is that only finitely many group elements translate a given compact set to meet itself.

= Dirichlet domain
{parent=Properly discontinuous group action}
{c}
{title2=$D_G(p)$}

= Dirichlet region
{synonym}
{c}

For a <group> of <isometries> acting properly discontinuously on the <hyperbolic plane>, and a point $p$ with trivial <point stabilizer>, the closed <Dirichlet domain> is
$$
D_G(p)=\{x:\rho(x,p)\le\rho(x,gp)\text{ for every }g\in G\}.
$$
It is the intersection of the closed half-planes bounded by <perpendicular bisectors> between $p$ and its other <group orbit> points. The <group orbit> is locally finite and the <hyperbolic plane> is a <proper metric space>, so every point has a nearest <group orbit> point. This shows that the translates of $D_G(p)$ cover the plane. Distinct interiors are disjoint, since being strictly closer to two different <group orbit> points is impossible. Boundary ties are allowed in the closed domain. Thus it is a geometric fundamental region.

= Gaussian-integer disc group with an ideal-square Dirichlet domain
{parent=Dirichlet domain}
{c}
{title2=$D=\{2(|x|+|y|)\le1+x^2+y^2\}\cap\mathbb D$}

Consider determinant-one <matrices> $\left(\begin{smallmatrix}u&v\\\overline v&\overline u\end{smallmatrix}\right)$ over <Gaussian integers> with $u+v\equiv1\pmod{2\mathbb Z[i]}$, modulo their common sign. They act on the <Poincare disc model> by fractional linear maps. Their origin-centered <Dirichlet domain> is
$$
D=\{x+iy: x^2+y^2<1,\ 2(|x|+|y|)\le1+x^2+y^2\}.
$$
For a nonidentity <matrix> set $N=|v|^2\ge1$ and $uv=m+in$. The <group orbit> point is $(m+in)/(N+1)$ and its bisector inequality is $2(mx+ny)\le N(1+x^2+y^2)$. Since $m^2+n^2=N(N+1)$, integrality gives $|m|,|n|\le N$, so the four inequalities defining $D$ imply all of them. They are necessary because the <matrices> with $u=1\pm i$, $v=\pm i$ realize the four combinations $m,n=\pm1$. The four sides are arcs of the unit-radius circles centered at $\pm1\pm i$, and the ideal vertices are $1,i,-1,-i$.

= Nielsen–Schreier theorem
{c}
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

Every <subgroup> of a <free group> is itself a <free group>. The theorem follows by representing the subgroup by a connected <covering graph> of a rose and choosing a maximal subtree.

= Nielsen–Schreier formula
{c}
{parent=Nielsen–Schreier theorem}

If $H$ has finite index $d$ in the free group $F_n$, then
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{rank}(H)=1+d(n-1).}
$$
Indeed, the $d$-sheeted <covering graph> of the rose with $n$ petals has $d$ vertices and $dn$ edges, so its <fundamental group> has rank $dn-d+1$.

= Rank-to-index limit for subgroups of a free group
{parent=Nielsen–Schreier formula}
{title2=$\operatorname{rank}(H)/[F_n:H]=n-1+1/[F_n:H]$}

For finite-index subgroups of a rank-$n$ <free group>, the <Nielsen–Schreier formula> gives the displayed quotient. Along any sequence whose finite indices tend to infinity, the quotient tends to $n-1$. Subtracting one from the rank before dividing gives the constant rank gradient $n-1$.

// Target: geometry-and-topology.bigb

= Schreier index-rank inequality
{c}
{parent=Nielsen–Schreier theorem}

If $H$ has finite index $d$ in a <finitely generated group> $G$, then <Schreier's lemma> gives
$$
\boxed{d(H)\leq 1+d\bigl(d(G)-1\bigr).}
$$
Equality holds when $G$ is a <free group> of finite rank.

= Finiteness of subgroups of a fixed finite index
{parent=Geometric group theory}

A <finitely generated group> has only finitely many <subgroups> of any prescribed <index of a subgroup> $n$. Every such subgroup is a point stabilizer for a transitive action on $n$ points, while a homomorphism from a finitely generated group to the finite <symmetric group> $S_n$ has only finitely many possible images of the generators.

= Growth function of a discrete metric space
{title2=$\beta_{X,x_0}$}
{parent=Geometric group theory}

For a locally finite discrete <metric space> $X$ with basepoint $x_0$, its growth function is
$$
\beta_{X,x_0}(r)=\#\{x\in X:d(x,x_0)\leq r\}.
$$
Changing the basepoint changes the argument by at most an additive constant. In a vertex-transitive metric space, including every <Cayley graph>, the growth function is exactly independent of the basepoint.

= Growth function of a finitely generated group
{title2=$\beta_{G,S}$}
{parent=Growth function of a discrete metric space}

= Growth functions of a finitely generated group
{synonym}

For a <finitely generated group> $G$ with finite generating set $S$, the growth function $\beta_{G,S}(r)$ is the number of group elements of <word metric> distance at most $r$ from the identity. Different finite generating sets change its scale only by multiplicative constants.

= Polynomial growth of a group
{parent=Growth function of a finitely generated group}

A <finitely generated group> has polynomial growth when, for some constants $C,D$, its <growth function of a finitely generated group> satisfies
$$
\beta_{G,S}(r)\leq C(1+r)^D.
$$
This property is independent of the finite generating set $S$.

= Polynomial growth of properly discontinuous isometry groups
{parent=Polynomial growth of a group}
{title2=$\beta_\Gamma(m)\leq |\Gamma_p|(1+Lm/\rho)^n$}

A finitely generated group of <isometries> acting <properly discontinuously> on a complete connected $n$-dimensional <Riemannian manifold> with nonnegative <Ricci curvature> has <polynomial growth of a group> of degree at most $n$. Choose $\rho>0$ so distinct points of an orbit are separated by more than $2\rho$. Let $L$ be the largest displacement of the base point by a generator. The radius-$m$ <word metric> ball maps into the metric ball of radius $Lm$, with fibers of size the finite stabilizer $|\Gamma_p|$. Disjoint radius-$\rho$ balls around these orbit points have equal volume and fit inside the ball of radius $Lm+\rho$. The <Bishop-Gromov inequality> bounds their number by $(1+Lm/\rho)^n$, giving the displayed estimate.

= First Betti-number bound from polynomial fundamental-group growth
{parent=Polynomial growth of properly discontinuous isometry groups}
{title2=$b_1(M)\leq\dim M$}

For a compact connected <Riemannian manifold> with nonnegative <Ricci curvature>, its finitely generated <fundamental group> acts freely and <properly discontinuously> on the complete <universal cover>. The preceding <polynomial growth of properly discontinuous isometry groups> bound applies. The degree-one <Hurewicz theorem> identifies first integral <homology> with the <abelianization> of the fundamental group. If its free rank is $b_1(M)$, this group maps onto $\mathbb Z^{b_1(M)}$. Choosing lifts of a basis gives at least a constant times $m^{b_1(M)}$ distinct elements in a word ball of radius a constant times $m$. The upper bound of order $m^{\dim M}$ forces the displayed inequality.

= Polynomial group growth from orbit packing
{parent=Polynomial growth of a group}
{title2=$|B_S(m)|\leq C(1+m)^n$}

A <properly discontinuous group action> by <isometries> of a <finitely generated group> on a complete $n$-dimensional <Riemannian manifold> of nonnegative <Ricci curvature> yields the displayed group growth bound. Distinct orbit points are uniformly separated, while a word of length $m$ moves a basepoint by at most $Dm$. Pack disjoint equal-volume small balls about the orbit points into $B(p,Dm+\varepsilon)$ and apply the <Bishop-Gromov inequality>. A finite <point stabilizer> contributes its order to the bound; freeness is unnecessary.

= One-scale virtual nilpotence theorem
{parent=Polynomial growth of a group}

For every $d>0$ there is $N(d)$ such that, if a group has a finite symmetric generating set $S$ containing the identity and
$$
|S^n|\leq n^d|S|
$$
for some $n\geq N(d)$, then there are $H\trianglelefteq C<G$ with $H\subseteq S^{\lfloor n/2\rfloor}$, $[G:C]=O_d(1)$, and $C/H$ nilpotent of class $O_d(1)$. A geometric-scale pigeonhole argument finds a large radius of small tripling, and the <Breuillard-Green-Tao structure theorem for approximate groups> supplies $H$ and $C$.

= Gromov's theorem on groups of polynomial growth
{c}
{parent=Polynomial growth of a group}
{wiki}

= Gromov theorem on groups of polynomial growth
{c}
{synonym}

Every finitely generated <group> of <polynomial growth of a group> has a <virtually nilpotent group> structure. Applying the <one-scale virtual nilpotence theorem> gives a finite normal subgroup with nilpotent quotient; centralizing that finite subgroup produces a nilpotent subgroup of finite index.

= Polynomial growth of a finitely generated two-step nilpotent group
{parent=Polynomial growth of a group}

Every finitely generated <two-step nilpotent group> has <polynomial growth of a group>. If $s_1,\ldots,s_k$ generate it, centrality of the commutators collects every word of length at most $n$ into
$$
s_1^{a_1}\cdots s_k^{a_k}\prod_{i<j}[s_i,s_j]^{b_{ij}},
$$
where $|a_i|\leq n$ and $|b_{ij}|=O(n^2)$. There are therefore only polynomially many possible collected words.

= Exponential growth of SL2 of Z
{title2=Exponential growth of $\operatorname{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$}
{c}
{parent=Growth function of a finitely generated group}

The matrices
$$
A=\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix},
\qquad
B=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\1&1\end{pmatrix}
$$
generate a <free monoid> in $\operatorname{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$. Hence the $2^n$ positive words of length $n$ are distinct and $\operatorname{SL}_2(\mathbb Z)$ has <exponential growth of a group>.

= Amenable group
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

A discrete group $G$ is amenable when it admits a left-invariant <finitely additive probability measure> on all subsets of $G$. Equivalently, it admits an <invariant mean> on the bounded real-valued functions on $G$.

= Reiter condition
{c}
{parent=Amenable group}
{title2=$\|\lambda(g)\mu_n-\mu_n\|_1\to0$}

= Reiter's condition
{c}
{synonym}

For a countable discrete <group>, the <Reiter condition> requires a sequence of <probability measures> in $\ell^1(G)$ asymptotically invariant under every left translation. It is equivalent to the existence of an <invariant mean> and a <Følner sequence>. Separation by bounded functions shows that amenability gives approximate invariance simultaneously on each finite set of translations; countability makes the successive finite tests into a sequence.

= Invariant mean
{parent=Amenable group}

An invariant mean on a group $G$ is a positive linear functional $m:\ell^\infty(G)\to\mathbb R$ with $m(1)=1$ and $m(gf)=m(f)$ for every $g\in G$. Applied to <indicator functions>, it is a left-invariant <finitely additive probability measure> on all subsets of $G$.

= Equidecomposable subsets under a group action
{parent=Amenable group}

For a group $G$ acting on a set $X$, subsets $A,B\subseteq X$ are $G$-equidecomposable when there are finite partitions $A=\bigsqcup_i A_i$ and $B=\bigsqcup_i B_i$ and elements $g_i\in G$ with $g_iA_i=B_i$ for every $i$.

= Paradoxical group action
{parent=Amenable group}

A $G$-set $X$ is paradoxical when it contains two disjoint subsets each $G$-equidecomposable with $X$. An invariant finitely additive probability measure rules out such a decomposition, because it would assign each copy the full measure of $X$.

= Nonamenability of a nonabelian free group
{parent=Amenable group}

The <free group> $F_2=\langle a,b\rangle$ is not an <amenable group>. Partitioning reduced words by their first letters gives two translated decompositions of $F_2$ whose invariance equations add to $2=1$, contradicting the existence of an <invariant mean>.

= A subgroup of an amenable group is amenable
{parent=Amenable group}

Every <subgroup> of an <amenable group> is amenable. One proof restricts an invariant mean after extending bounded functions from the subgroup to the whole group along a set of coset representatives. Consequently, a group containing a nonamenable subgroup is nonamenable.

= Virtually abelian groups are amenable
{parent=Amenable group}

Every <abelian group> is amenable, and amenability is preserved by finite extensions. Hence every group with an abelian <finite-index subgroup> is an <amenable group>.

= Følner condition
{c}
{parent=Amenable group}

A <finitely generated group> $G$ with finite generating set $S$ satisfies the Følner condition when, for every $\varepsilon>0$, there is a nonempty finite subset $F\subseteq G$ such that
$$
\frac{|sF\mathbin\triangle F|}{|F|}<\varepsilon
\qquad(s\in S).
$$
The condition is independent of the finite generating set.

= Følner sequence
{c}
{parent=Følner condition}

A Følner sequence is a sequence of nonempty finite subsets $F_n\subseteq G$ such that $|gF_n\mathbin\triangle F_n|/|F_n|\to0$ for every $g\in G$.

= Layer-cake extraction of Følner sets
{parent=Følner sequence}

For a finitely supported nonnegative function $\mu$ of sum one, let $F_t=\{x:\mu(x)>t\}$. Then $\int_0^\infty|F_t|\,dt=1$ and $\int_0^\infty|F_t\mathbin\triangle gF_t|\,dt=\|\mu-\lambda(g)\mu\|_1$. If the sum of these latter integrals over a finite set of translations is less than $\varepsilon$, some nonempty level set has the sum of its relative boundaries less than $\varepsilon$. This converts the <Reiter condition> into finite <Følner sets> without assuming that the <group> is finitely generated.

= Følner sequence for the integers
{c}
{parent=Følner sequence}

The intervals $F_n=\{-n,\ldots,n\}$ form a <Følner sequence> for the additive group $\mathbb Z$, because
$$
\frac{|(F_n+1)\mathbin\triangle F_n|}{|F_n|}=\frac{2}{2n+1}\longrightarrow0.
$$

= Følner condition implies amenability
{c}
{parent=Følner condition}

Normalized counting measures on a <Følner sequence> have a convergent subnet in the compact product $[0,1]^{\mathcal P(G)}$. The limit is a left-invariant finitely additive probability measure, so every group satisfying the <Følner condition> is an <amenable group>.

= Amenability of a free product
{parent=Amenable group}

For nontrivial groups $A,B$, the <free product> $A*B$ is amenable exactly in the exceptional case $A\cong B\cong C_2$. In that case it is the <infinite dihedral group>, hence a <virtually cyclic group> and amenable. In every other case the <normal form theorem for a free product> and the <ping-pong lemma> produce a subgroup isomorphic to $F_2$; the fact that <a subgroup of an amenable group is amenable> then obstructs amenability.

= Length function
{parent=Geometric group theory}
{wiki}

A group length function $L:G\to[0,\infty)$ satisfies $L(1)=0$, $L(g^{-1})=L(g)$ and $L(gh)\leq L(g)+L(h)$. Word length for a chosen generating set is an example; <Coxeter length> uses the simple reflections.
